{
  "rationale": "Evidence from the iteration 0 race: every development track ended off-track (oval 20/20, s-bend 20/20, slalom 20/20). Progress was only 0.115 on the oval and 0.04 and 0.035 on the s-bend and slalom. Each run logged one line-loss event, and max_error sat at about 0.060, which is the off-track limit. Steering oscillation was low (0.04 to 0.07), so the robot is not jittering. It drifts steadily away from the line, most likely because it is too fast to follow the bends: wheel slip and lag at base_speed 1.6 leave the yaw rate behind the curvature. Evaluator feedback also flagged base_speed 1.6 as the main risk to completion. Change: reduce base_speed from 1.6 to 1.3 and keep every other field identical (kp 4.0, ki 0.05, kd 0.05, sensor weights and geometry unchanged). This is a single-group change, so any effect on progress or max_error can be attributed to speed. Expected result: fewer off-track exits and higher progress on the oval and s-bend, with max_error below 0.06. Completion may still be marginal, since 1.3 is slower than the 1.5 m/s average needed to finish in 3 s. Hypotheses to test: (1) the baseline failures are caused by speed-induced under-steering, so progress rises and max_error falls at 1.3; (2) if progress rises but completion is still missed, the next step is a small kp or kd increase rather than more speed reduction; (3) if line_loss_events stays at 1, test line_loss = search_right on the slalom. Measurements requested: all fields, so that completion_time, max_error and steering_oscillation can separate speed effects from steering effects.",
